# Docker
#
# docker-compose.override.yml is deliberately NOT ignored. It used to be, on the
# usual assumption that an override file holds one developer's personal tweaks.
# It no longer does: docker-compose.yml is the production topology and the
# override carries the whole development setup — source bind mounts, --reload,
# published ports, host Ollama. Ignoring it would mean a fresh clone ran the
# production configuration locally and nobody could work.
#
# For personal, uncommitted tweaks use an extra file and an explicit flag:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.override.yml -f my.yml up -d
docker-compose.local.yml

